<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.crumley.switchboard-runner — the night shift's clock on this Mac.

  THIS FILE IS NOT LOADED FROM WHERE IT IS STOWED. It lives here, in
  ~/.config/switchboard/, beside runner.env, and `install.sh` copies it to
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.crumley.switchboard-runner.plist. Two reasons, and
  the second is the load-bearing one:

    * Apple documents nothing either way about launchd following a symlink in
      ~/Library/LaunchAgents. launchd.plist(5) and the (now retired) Daemons and
      Services Programming Guide describe the directory and the file format and
      say nothing about symlinks; `launchctl bootstrap` takes a path and
      resolves it, but the per-user domain's own scan at login is not specified.
      An agent that works until the next reboot and then silently does not is
      the worst failure available here, so this does not rely on an answer
      nobody has published.

    * Keeping the live path out of the stow tree is the same trade this repo
      already makes for ~/.claude/settings.json: the tracked file and the live
      file can never be the same path, so nothing that rewrites the live one can
      dirty the checkout.

  There is no username and no absolute home path anywhere below, because
  launchd expands nothing in ProgramArguments — not `~`, not `$HOME`. The job is
  therefore run through `/bin/sh -lc`, which is a login shell: it reads
  ~/.profile, which discovers Homebrew and puts ~/.local/bin and ~/bin on PATH.
  Ghostty is started the same way in this repo and for the same reason (see
  AGENTS.md, "Terminal"): Spotlight/LaunchServices hand a process a system-only
  PATH. The alternative — having the installer render the absolute path into a
  copy of this file — was rejected because it puts machine state into a
  generated file and makes "is the installed copy current?" a fuzzy question
  instead of a byte comparison.

  StandardOutPath / StandardErrorPath are deliberately absent: launchd does not
  expand `~` in them either, and the wrapper writes ~/Library/Logs/switchboard-runner.log
  itself. What escapes the wrapper entirely (it could not even be started) shows
  up as the last exit status in `launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.crumley.switchboard-runner`.

  KeepAlive is deliberately absent too. This is a one-shot command that exits 0
  most ticks; KeepAlive would restart it in a tight loop.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.crumley.switchboard-runner</string>

	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/sh</string>
		<string>-lc</string>
		<string>exec "$HOME/bin/switchboard-runner"</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Every five minutes. The VM's timer ticks once a minute because the VM
	     is woken for one job and should notice it and stop again quickly; this
	     Mac is never woken, the ff2k slots are on the hour, and the cadence
	     calls them "when to sit down", not deadlines. Five minutes keeps a
	     gameday check well inside its 90-minute window and cuts the empty
	     polls (a login shell, a bun process, a keychain read and one request
	     to the Worker each) from 1,440 a day to 288. The runner's own lock is
	     what keeps a 45-minute ff2k run from being started on top of itself. -->
	<key>StartInterval</key>
	<integer>300</integer>

	<!-- Run one tick as soon as the agent is bootstrapped, so `launchctl
	     bootstrap` is immediately verifiable rather than five minutes of silence. -->
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Background: lower CPU and I/O priority, and no App Nap surprises. This
	     runs while Ryan is using the machine. -->
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
